INT. COUNTY HOSPITAL - POLIO WARD - NIGHT

Iron lungs line the room like a row of steel whales. Green light. A ceiling fan turns slowly. A radio murmurs a dance tune from a distant city.

DR. ALISTAIR VANCE-HOLLOWAY (50s, tall, courteous, bow tie loosened) pauses at the foot of a bed with a chart. NURSE BERNADETTE OKONJO (40s, starched cap, quick hands) adjusts a dial on the nearest machine. MRS. THEODORA LINDQUIST (60s, wool coat, handbag in both hands) sits very straight on a visitor's chair. CASPER WU (20s, medical student, borrowed white coat) stands by the door, taking notes. CHAPLAIN AUGUSTUS DELACROIX-BENNETT (70s, soft voice, stole around his neck) kneels in prayer at a bedside.

He reads the chart a long time. Mrs. Lindquist watches him. Nurse Okonjo does not.

MRS. LINDQUIST
He seems comfortable.

DR. VANCE-HOLLOWAY
He is resting.

MRS. LINDQUIST
That is not the same thing.

DR. VANCE-HOLLOWAY
No. It is not.

She nods, grateful for the honesty. He closes the chart.

CASPER
(low)
Doctor, the pressure reading on bed six--

DR. VANCE-HOLLOWAY
Later, Mr. Wu.

CASPER
Yes, sir.

Okonjo crosses to the window. The blinds are drawn. She tugs a cord and the slats rise. Moonlight spills onto the machine, silvering the boy inside, who sleeps with his mouth a little open.

NURSE OKONJO
His color is better.

MRS. LINDQUIST
Is it?

NURSE OKONJO
Pink in the cheeks.

Dr. Vance-Holloway steps closer. He lifts the boy's wrist. A long moment.

DR. VANCE-HOLLOWAY
Nurse. The oxygen.

NURSE OKONJO
Already, Doctor.

She is turning a valve. He does not say what she did, and she does not say what she saw. Casper looks from one to the other, understanding nothing, then too much.

CASPER
Doctor?

DR. VANCE-HOLLOWAY
Fetch the Chaplain.

CASPER
He's here.

DR. VANCE-HOLLOWAY
Then bring him.

Casper crosses the room. The Chaplain looks up from his prayer before he arrives.

CHAPLAIN DELACROIX-BENNETT
I know.

He rises, creaking, and takes Mrs. Lindquist's hand. She stares at it without understanding. Then the radio gives a bright trumpet note, and Mrs. Lindquist rises, breath catching, and lets the handbag fall.

MRS. LINDQUIST
No.

DR. VANCE-HOLLOWAY
Mrs. Lindquist.

MRS. LINDQUIST
He was pink. She said he was pink.

NURSE OKONJO
(quietly)
He was, ma'am.

Mrs. Lindquist crosses to the bed. Puts a hand on the cold steel. The machine wheezes its steady wheeze, pushing air into a chest that no longer asks.

DR. VANCE-HOLLOWAY
I am so sorry.

She does not turn. Nobody speaks. In the corner, the radio plays on, a waltz, cheerful and absurd. Nurse Okonjo crosses and switches it off, and the silence that follows is the loudest thing in the ward.
